<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Hakam I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 770–<a href="/years/822">822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Al-Hakam I of Córdoba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hakam I was the Umayyad emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> from 796 to 822. His reign was marked by harsh repression of internal opposition, including a massacre of notables at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 797 and the crushing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-arrabal">Revolt of the Arrabal</a> in Córdoba in 818, after which the rebellious suburb was razed and its inhabitants expelled. He strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s army with professional and slave troops.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hakam-i.jpg" alt="Al-Hakam I" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_al_hakam_i_17521.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu al-As al-Hakam ibn Hisham ibn Abd al-Rahman was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-i-of-cordoba">Hisham I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and a concubine named Zokhrouf; his older brother had died young. He was a complete contrast to his father. Chroniclers describe a tall, dark, slim man who led his troops into battle himself, enjoyed wine and women in quantity, cultivated his gifts as a poet and orator and promoted learning. The religious elite, who preferred the near-asceticism of his father, were not won over by him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When he came to power in 796 he was challenged by his uncles Sulayman and Abdallah, sons of Abd ar-Rahman I. After a four-year struggle Sulayman was captured and executed, and his head was paraded on a pike through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Abdallah took his sons to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> at Aix-la-Chapelle to seek aid, without result, and was pardoned. Al-Hakam gave him lordship over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> with a rich pension, on condition that he stay there, and married two of his sisters to Abdallah's sons.</p><p>He spent much of his reign putting down rebellions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, Saragossa and Mérida, and the uprisings twice reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. When a plot to replace him with his cousin Muhammad ibn al-Kasim was exposed, seventy-two nobles were executed and crucified along the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a>. He raised new taxes to pay for his campaigns, which the clerics decried as un-Islamic, and he tolerated the public sale of wine for revenue. He formed a bodyguard, the Al-Haras, led by the Christian count Rabi, and a troop of Frankish warriors.</p><p>In the spring of 818 the townsfolk of the suburb of Shaqunda, across the Roman bridge, rose after a palace guard killed a townsman, and stormed the city. His cousin Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah surprised the rebels from behind, and for three days the suburb was given over to the troops. The emir then had three hundred leading citizens crucified and gave the surviving inhabitants, thousands of Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, four days to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Some went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Hakam I died in 822 after ruling for 26 years. By his wife Halawah he had five children, among them Abd ar-Rahman II, who succeeded him as emir. His concubine Ajab founded a leper colony in the suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, funded by the estate of Munyat Ajab, and another concubine, Mut'a, established a cemetery that still existed in the 10th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Hakam I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Al-Hakam I</b> (796–822) faced the most serious opposition from his own subjects: according to tradition he had notables of Toledo massacred in 797, and in 818 he crushed the Revolt of the Arrabal, a rising of the southern suburb of Córdoba, razed the quarter and expelled its inhabitants,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=803" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4431 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Al-Hakam I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=803">What was here in 803 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
